a:43:{i:0;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:51:"Surviving Disaster Speaker Series – register now!";s:4:"link";s:87:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/09/surviving-disaster-speaker-series-register-now/";s:11:"description";s:470:"<div>The College of the Environment in partnership with The Graduate School and the UW Alumni Association is pleased to present the fall speaker series Surviving Disaster: Natural Hazards &amp; Resilient Communities. Join us for a series of discussions centered around natural disasters &mdash; like earthquakes, tsunamis, landslides and hurricanes &mdash; and how they can affect the lives and livelihoods of people here in the Pacific Northwest and beyond.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:11:"Joe Eastham";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-09-01 18:40:55";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:1;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:80:"Exploring wilderness in one of the lower forty-eight’s most untamed landscapes";s:4:"link";s:118:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/08/exploring-wilderness-in-one-of-the-lower-forty-eights-most-untamed-landscapes/";s:11:"description";s:428:"<div>What is wilderness?
 As we sit at our computers or scroll through on tablets or smart phones, perhaps we picture the opposite of our current locales&mdash;mountainous terrain, soaring evergreens, and a variety of critters that depend on each other to maintain balanced ecosystems. Maybe thoughts of flora and fauna, untamed and unexposed to an otherwise modern, industrialized, human-centric world swirl around.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-08-21 00:15:49";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:2;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:59:"UW researchers model tsunami hazards on the Northwest coast";s:4:"link";s:100:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/08/uw-researchers-model-tsunami-hazards-on-the-northwest-coast/";s:11:"description";s:527:"<div>Recent press coverage and conversations on social media have been a good reminder for Pacific Northwesterners that they live in a seismically active region. Stretching from northern California to British Columbia, the Cascadia subduction zone could slip at any time, causing a powerful earthquake and triggering a tsunami that would impact coastal communities.
Scientists from multiple disciplines at the University of Washington and other institutions are working to learn&nbsp;more about this natural hazard.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-08-20 17:20:07";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:3;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:59:"UW oceanographers explore recently erupted deep-sea volcano";s:4:"link";s:100:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/08/uw-oceanographers-explore-recently-erupted-deep-sea-volcano/";s:11:"description";s:472:"<div>This spring, seafloor seismometers connected to shore by a new Internet cable showed that the 3,600-foot-tall underwater Axial Volcano started shaking April 24, 2015 and shook continuously for several days. University of Washington oceanographers visited the deep-ocean volcano in late July and parts of the seafloor were still warm, giving the team a glimpse into the changes that happened around the the mile-deep volcano 300 miles off the Oregon coast.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-08-07 22:51:31";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:4;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:78:"Earthquake early warning system funding awarded to UW, West Coast universities";s:4:"link";s:118:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/07/earthquake-early-warning-system-funding-awarded-to-uw-west-coast-universities/";s:11:"description";s:600:"<div>A recent article&nbsp;published by The New Yorker has resulted in&nbsp;widespread talks&nbsp;and panic about the risk of a mega-earthquake off the Pacific Northwest coast. While the seismic hazard is real, the article&rsquo;s tone may have been overly fatalistic&nbsp;and didn&rsquo;t highlight&nbsp;earthquake preparedness&nbsp;tools that are now being developed. The U.S. Geological Survey announced today that $5 million will go to the University of Washington and three other institutions to help transition the ShakeAlert earthquake early warning system to a public-facing tool.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-07-30 17:48:06";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:5;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:58:"JISAO scientists play critical role in ocean health XPRIZE";s:4:"link";s:116:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/07/jisao-scientists-play-critical-role-in-xprize-to-combat-ocean-acidification/";s:11:"description";s:530:"<div>Sunburst Sensors, based out of Missoula, MT, grabbed the two top spots in the Wendy Schmidt Ocean Health XPRIZE, receiving a $1.5 million award for advancing scientist&rsquo;s ability to measure ocean chemistry as it relates to ocean acidification. Designed as a competition to spur innovation, teams from around the globe competed to develop the most promising technologies in two categories: a device that is easy to use and cost effective and a device that is highly accurate when tracking ocean acidification.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:10:"John Meyer";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-07-21 15:23:52";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:6;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:101:"College of the Environment students, researchers at sea to learn more about deep-sea volcano eruption";s:4:"link";s:141:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/07/college-of-the-environment-students-researchers-at-sea-to-learn-more-about-deep-sea-volcano-eruption/";s:11:"description";s:509:"<div>When the Axial Seamount, an&nbsp;underwater volcano, erupted off the coast of Oregon in&nbsp;April, researchers knew within minutes that something spectacular was happening more than 300 miles offshore. Precision hardware installed by the University of Washington last summer let scientists see its effects almost instantly from shore.&shy;&shy;&nbsp;A team of researchers, engineers and students is now at sea working t&shy;o maintain that equipment and assess the volcano&rsquo;s aftermath.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-07-15 20:25:25";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:7;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:89:"UW oceanographers wire up an undersea volcano to study Earth’s most extreme environment";s:4:"link";s:127:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/07/uw-oceanographers-wire-up-an-undersea-volcano-to-study-earths-most-extreme-environment/";s:11:"description";s:701:"<div>Several hundred miles off the coast of Oregon and Washington, there&rsquo;s an undersea volcano known as Axial Seamount.&nbsp;Thanks to a set of high-tech instruments installed last summer by a team of scientists, many from&nbsp;the College of the Environment, the deep sea is online and oceanographers&nbsp;were able to observe an&nbsp;eruption of the Axial Volcano in April 2015. PBS NewsHour&rsquo;s Hari Sreenivasan recently spoke with the School of Oceanography&lsquo;s John Delaney and Debbie&nbsp;Kelley about their team&rsquo;s deep sea observatory, including a network of sensors, moorings, and cameras that collect&nbsp;troves of new information about this underwater world.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-07-08 18:36:06";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:8;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:76:"Scientists weigh in on carbon emissions’ effect on future ocean conditions";s:4:"link";s:113:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/07/scientists-weigh-in-on-carbon-dioxides-effect-on-future-ocean-conditions/";s:11:"description";s:476:"<div>Ahead of major climate talks at COP21 this year in Paris, scientists are offering insights to the far-reaching effects of rising carbon dioxide levels on the ocean. Spearheaded by the Oceans 2015 Initiative, which brought together 22 scientists and policy experts from nine different countries, the results were published this week in the journal Science and focus on how warming waters, rising seas, and ocean acidification drive changes to the global ocean.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:10:"John Meyer";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-07-06 16:24:51";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:9;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:96:"Researcher at UW’s Olympic Natural Resources Center helps pinpoint massive harmful algal bloom";s:4:"link";s:138:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/06/researcher-at-the-uws-olympic-natural-resources-center-helps-pinpoint-massive-harmful-algal-bloom/";s:11:"description";s:476:"<div>The algal bloom that shut down several shellfish fisheries along the West Coast earlier this year has developed into the largest and most severe in a decade or more&mdash;stretching from at least central California to as far north as Alaska. UW research analyst Anthony Odell is part of a NOAA-led team of harmful algae experts who are surveying the extent of the patch and searching for the swirling eddies that can become toxic to marine mammals and humans.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-06-25 21:00:45";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:10;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:65:"What does it take to catalogue every glacier in a mountain range?";s:4:"link";s:105:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/06/what-does-it-take-to-catalogue-every-glacier-in-a-mountain-range/";s:11:"description";s:414:"<div>After days of waiting around in Port Angeles, Washington, Earth and Space Sciences&rsquo; T.J. Fudge finally got some good news: a helicopter would be able to drop him and another researcher into the wilderness of Olympic National Park. Fudge didn&rsquo;t know it yet, but nasty weather would prevent the helicopter from returning to pick them up, leaving the scientists no choice but to hike out.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:16:"Jennifer Davison";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-06-18 18:24:18";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:11;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:54:"Warmer, lower-oxygen oceans will shift marine habitats";s:4:"link";s:94:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/06/warmer-lower-oxygen-oceans-will-shift-marine-habitats/";s:11:"description";s:461:"<div>A research team that includes scientists from the College of the Environment&rsquo;s School of Oceanography found that warmer ocean temperatures and decreasing levels of dissolved oxygen as a result of climate change will increase metabolic stress on marine animals. These new findings suggest that warmer water will speed up animals&rsquo; metabolic need for oxygen, but will simultaneously hold less of the oxygen needed to fuel their bodies.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-06-05 16:36:29";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:12;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:79:"UW’s Deborah Kelley publishes atlas of seafloor volcanoes and deep-ocean life";s:4:"link";s:117:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/05/uws-deborah-kelley-publishes-atlas-of-seafloor-volcanoes-and-deep-ocean-life/";s:11:"description";s:592:"<div>A University of Washington oceanographer has helped create the first photographic atlas of the ocean floor. &ldquo;Discovering the Deep: A Photographic Atlas of the Seafloor and Ocean Crust&rdquo; (Cambridge University Press, 2015) was almost a decade in the making and contains more than 500 original illustrations and color photos, and access to online educational resources and high-definition videos.
Its pages contain a history of deep-sea science and a global tour of the volcanoes, hot springs, rocks and animals that exist in extreme environments in the ocean depths.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:10:"John Meyer";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-05-19 20:54:30";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:13;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:64:"New knowledge and technology help scientists track harmful algae";s:4:"link";s:107:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/05/new-knowledge-and-technology-help-scientists-track-poisonous-algae/";s:11:"description";s:477:"<div>Though the waters of Puget Sound are full of beneficial algae, which provide oxygen, food, and shelter for other creatures, it&rsquo;s the nasty ones that usually make the news, when they "bloom" into toxic pools, harming fish and humans. Now, researchers working with Washington Sea Grant have started to narrow in on harmful algae&rsquo;s behaviors, and are developing some slick techniques that they hope will lead to much more effective detection and monitoring.</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:16:"Jennifer Davison";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-05-12 20:44:13";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:14;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:63:"Seafloor sensors record possible eruption of underwater volcano";s:4:"link";s:104:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/05/seafloor-sensors-record-possible-eruption-of-underwater-volcano/";s:11:"description";s:408:"<div>Thanks to a set of high-tech instruments installed last summer by a team of scientists, many at the College of the Environment, the deep sea is online and scientists were able to observe the eruption of the Axial Volcano on April 23. About 300 miles offshore from Astoria, Oregon, and one mile deep, the data collected flowed back to the land at the speed of light through fiber optic cable.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:10:"John Meyer";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-05-01 17:19:48";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:15;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:59:"Tidal tugs on Teflon faults drive slow-slipping earthquakes";s:4:"link";s:100:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/04/tidal-tugs-on-teflon-faults-drive-slow-slipping-earthquakes/";s:11:"description";s:517:"<div>Unknown to most people, the Pacific Northwest experiences a magnitude-6.6 earthquake about once a year. The reason nobody notices is that the movement happens slowly and deep underground, in a part of the fault whose behavior, known as slow-slip, was only recently discovered.
