tionship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:18;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:91:"Kill two birds with one dead tree? Beetle-killed pines could fuel machines instead of fires";s:4:"link";s:131:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/03/kill-two-birds-with-one-dead-tree-beetle-killed-pines-could-fuel-machines-instead-of-fires/";s:11:"description";s:417:"<div>Across western North America you can see them: hills blanketed with swaths of red and gray trees. These dead and dying stands of pine, aspen, and fir, totaling around 42 million acres&mdash;roughly the size of the state of Washington&mdash;are victims of bark beetles. And the dry, decaying trees that the beetles leave in their wake are not just eyesores; they also fuel hotter and larger wildfires.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:16:"Jennifer Davison";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-03-16 17:18:07";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:19;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:79:"UW’s Friday Harbor Labs prove to be a prime spot to study ocean acidification";s:4:"link";s:117:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/03/uws-friday-harbor-labs-prove-to-be-a-prime-spot-to-study-ocean-acidification/";s:11:"description";s:456:"<div>For more than a century, scientists at UW have utilized Friday Harbor Laboratories&rsquo; unique location on the shores of the Puget Sound to study a variety of marine species. With the debut of the Ocean Acidification Environmental Laboratory in 2011, research at Friday Harbor Labs expanded into monitoring the water&rsquo;s pH and dissolved oxygen levels, total alkalinity, effects of ocean acidification, and strategies for adaptation.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Author":3:{s:4:"name";s:19:"Kelly Knickerbocker";s:4:"link";N;s:5:"email";N;}s:4:"date";s:19:"2015-03-12 21:42:07";s:10:"enclosures";a:1:{i:0;O:19:"SimplePie_Enclosure":27:{s:7:"bitrate";N;s:8:"captions";N;s:10:"categories";N;s:8:"channels";N;s:9:"copyright";N;s:7:"credits";N;s:11:"description";N;s:8:"duration";N;s:10:"expression";N;s:9:"framerate";N;s:7:"handler";N;s:6:"hashes";N;s:6:"height";N;s:10:"javascript";N;s:8:"keywords";N;s:4:"lang";N;s:6:"length";N;s:4:"link";N;s:6:"medium";N;s:6:"player";N;s:7:"ratings";N;s:12:"restrictions";a:1:{i:0;O:21:"SimplePie_Restriction":3:{s:12:"relationship";s:5:"allow";s:4:"type";N;s:5:"value";s:7:"default";}}s:12:"samplingrate";N;s:10:"thumbnails";N;s:5:"title";N;s:4:"type";N;s:5:"width";N;}}}i:20;O:8:"stdClass":6:{s:5:"title";s:90:"Washington Sea Grant explores relative sea-level rise to prepare Pacific Coast communities";s:4:"link";s:131:"https://environment.uw.edu/news/2015/02/washington-sea-grant-explores-relative-sea-level-rise-to-prepare-pacific-coast-communities/";s:11:"description";s:495:"<div>Washington Sea Grant, a unit in the College of the Environment, works to restore and protect marine environments through addressing important issues, providing better tools for marine management, and supporting strategic partnerships within the marine community. Coastal hazards specialist and resident geologist Ian Miller embodies this approach, and is studying changes in local sea level over time in order to help coastal communities plan for rising seas along their shores.&nbsp;</div>";s:6:"author";O:16:"SimplePie_Aut